    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0012219 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0012219">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0011123"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>An erythematous eruption commonly associated with drug reactions or infection and characterized by inflammatory nodules that are usually tender, multiple, and bilateral.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <terms:creator rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199"/>
        <terms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2013-03-30T06:57:07Z</terms:date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>SNOMEDCT_US:32861005</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>UMLS:C0014743</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:id>HP:0012219</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:comment>The nodules of erythema nodosum are located predominantly on the shins with less common occurrence on the thighs and forearms. They undergo characteristic color changes ending in temporary bruise-like areas, and usually subside in 3-6 weeks without scarring or atrophy.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Erythema nodosum</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0012219"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>An erythematous eruption commonly associated with drug reactions or infection and characterized by inflammatory nodules that are usually tender, multiple, and bilateral.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199"/>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0012227 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0012227">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0000795"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Narrowing of the urethra associated with inflammation or scar tissue.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <terms:creator rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199"/>
        <terms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2013-03-30T07:37:46Z</terms:date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>SNOMEDCT_US:236647003</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>SNOMEDCT_US:76618002</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>UMLS:C0041974</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
        <oboInOwl:id>HP:0012227</oboInOwl:id>
        <rdfs:comment>A urethral stricture may result from surgery, disease, or injury. The term should not be used for a congenital narrowing of the urethra (Consider the term Urethral stenosis, HP:0008661). All strictures result from injury to the epithelium of the urethra or underlying corpus spongiosum, which ultimately causes fibrosis during the healing process. The pathological changes associated with strictures show that the normal pseudostratified columnar epithelium is replaced with squamous metaplasia. Small tears in this metaplastic tissue result in urinary extravasation, which causes a fibrotic reaction within the spongiosum. At the time of injury, this fibrosis can be asymptomatic; however, over time, the fibrotic process can cause further narrowing of the lumen of the urethra, resulting in symptomatic obstructive voiding.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Urethral stricture</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0012227"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Narrowing of the urethra associated with inflammation or scar tissue.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199"/>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:24346008</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0012228 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0012228">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0002315"/>
