    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0020301 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0020301">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0009811"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A functional anomaly of the joint that connects the upper and the lower arm.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <terms:creator rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199"/>
        <terms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2025-11-19T07:45:49Z</terms:date>
        <rdfs:label>Abnormal elbow physiology</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0020317 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0020317">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0011004"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A structural anomaly of an artery that originates from the abdominal aorta.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <terms:creator rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199"/>
        <terms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2026-02-24T07:16:14Z</terms:date>
        <rdfs:comment>The abdominal aorta extends from the T12 vertebra and terminates at the L4 vertebra. It gives off anterior, lateral, and posterior branches that supply the abdominal viscera and vertebrae at this level, eventually dividing into the common iliac arteries. Upaired branches (and their typical location) comprise the celiac artery (T12), the superior mesenteric artery (L1), the inferior mesenteric artery (L3), and the median sacral artery. Paired branches comprise the middle adrenal arteries, the renal arteries (L1-2), the gonadal arteries (between L2 and L3), the inferior phrenic arteries, and the lumbar arteries. The abdominal aorta bifurcates into right and left common iliac arteries at L4.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Abnormal abdominal aorta branch artery morphology</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0020317"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>A structural anomaly of an artery that originates from the abdominal aorta.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboInOwl:hasDbXref>PMID:39350866</oboInOwl:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0020330 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0020330">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0003111"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>Any deviation from the normal concentration of inorganic anion in the blood circulation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <terms:creator rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199"/>
        <terms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2026-03-09T07:31:41Z</terms:date>
        <rdfs:comment>Inorganic anions are negatively charged mineral ions that do not contain carbon-hydrogen bonds. The major clinically important anions are chloride, bicarbonate, and phosphate. Additionally, sulfate, nitrate, nitrite, fluoride, and bromide are measured for certain indications.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Abnormal circulating inorganic anion concentration</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0020347 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0020347">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0011013"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>A deviation from the normal concentration of a monosaccharide in the blood circulation.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <terms:creator rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199"/>
        <terms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime">2026-05-24T08:11:09Z</terms:date>
        <rdfs:comment>The simplest carbohydrates are called monosaccharides, or simple sugars. They are the building blocks (monomers) for the synthesis of polymers or complex carbohydrates. Monosaccharides are classified based on the number of carbons in the molecule. General categories are identified using a prefix that indicates the number of carbons and the suffix -ose, which indicates a saccharide; for example, triose (three carbons), tetrose (four carbons), pentose (five carbons), and hexose (six carbons). The hexose D-glucose is the most abundant monosaccharide in nature.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>Abnormal circulating monosaccharide concentration</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0020350 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0020350">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/HP_0032180"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The concentration of one or more vitamins is outside the reference range.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <terms:creator rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0736-9199"/>
